"""Reviewed teaching notes; stable IDs retain the supplied question and grading keys."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
GROUPS=[]
def note(ids,idea,cue):
 for i in ids: GROUPS.append((i,'✅ '+idea+'\n💡 '+cue))
note([1,28,181],'Double tagging exploits native-VLAN handling: the first switch removes the outer native tag, exposing the inner target VLAN tag. Keeping user traffic out of the native VLAN reduces this opportunity.','Native VLAN = untagged traffic; use an unused VLAN for it.')
note([2,12,42,49],'802.1X controls network access at a port. The client is the supplicant, the switch or AP is the authenticator, and an authentication server checks credentials.','Supplicant asks; authenticator relays; server decides.')
note([3,52,130,203],'HSRP failover follows this sequence: the active router fails; the standby stops receiving hello messages; the standby takes the active role; the new active assumes the virtual IP and MAC. Hosts keep using the same gateway.','Failure → missing hellos → takeover → virtual addresses.')
note([4,24,65,151],'Automatic trunk negotiation can let an attacker establish a trunk. Configure access ports explicitly, configure real trunks manually, disable unnecessary DTP negotiation, and keep the native VLAN unused by clients.','Access ports stay access; trunks are deliberate.')
note([5,10,30,36],'Authorization decides which resources and actions an authenticated user is allowed to use. A PASS or FAIL response communicates whether a requested service is permitted.','Authentication = who; authorization = allowed actions; accounting = activity log.')
note([6],'PAgP is Cisco proprietary and negotiates an EtherChannel. LACP is the standards-based alternative. Static on mode bundles without negotiating.','PAgP: desirable/auto. LACP: active/passive.')
note([7,8,29,50],'The show command determines the level of detail: etherchannel summary lists bundles and member flags; interfaces port-channel1 reports the logical interface, including reliability; etherchannel port-channel shows bundle membership; interfaces fa0/2 etherchannel examines that physical member.','Summary = overview; port-channel interface = logical link; physical interface = member.')
note([9,35,38,54,64,135,179],'A VPN builds a secure tunnel across an untrusted network. A next-generation firewall inspects and controls traffic using application awareness. NAC controls admission and can use AAA. These products can integrate, so these classroom statements describe their main roles rather than every possible feature.','VPN = tunnel; NGFW = inspect; NAC = admission. Preserve the supplied key for this wording.')
note([11,17,44,79,188,209],'GLBP is Cisco proprietary and combines gateway redundancy with load sharing. Multiple forwarding routers can carry traffic while presenting a shared virtual gateway to hosts; the IPv6 variant serves IPv6.','GLBP = Gateway Load Balancing Protocol.')
note([13,16,43,69],'A data breach exposes or steals confidential information. A distributed denial-of-service attack uses many devices to overwhelm a service and reduce availability. The statements become false when these definitions are swapped.','Breach attacks confidentiality; DDoS attacks availability.')
note([14,22],'IRDP uses ICMP Router Discovery advertisements and solicitations to help IPv4 hosts discover routers. This is different from HSRP and VRRP election mechanisms.','The I in IRDP connects to ICMP router discovery.')
note([15],'The exhibit identifies the router as active, which is the HSRP router currently forwarding traffic. A configured priority does not by itself prove a router is active; read its operational state.','State tells what it is doing; priority influences election.')
note([18,45,48,146,175],'Authentication checks identity using credentials such as a username and password. Local AAA stores credentials on the device; server-based AAA centralizes checking through RADIUS or TACACS+.','Who are you? Authentication. What may you do? Authorization.')
note([19],'CDP and LLDP advertise device information to directly connected neighbors. Disable them on interfaces where discovery is unnecessary to reduce information exposure.','Discovery helps neighbors, including unwanted neighbors.')
note([20,21,32,39,47,74,129,136,138,178],'VRRP is an open-standard gateway redundancy protocol; VRRPv2 serves IPv4. HSRP is Cisco proprietary. VRRP elects a master and uses backup routers; HSRP uses active and standby roles.','VRRP = open standard; HSRP = Cisco. Do not swap those descriptions.')
note([23,33,71,72,81,131,149,176,177,182],'First-hop redundancy gives hosts a virtual default gateway backed by multiple routers. HSRP uses an active router and a standby; hosts use the virtual IP and virtual MAC, allowing failover without changing their gateway setting.','Hosts point to the virtual gateway, not one physical router.')
note([25],'SSH encrypts remote management traffic and credentials. Telnet sends its session without encryption, making it inappropriate for secure remote administration.','SSH secures the shell; Telnet is plaintext.')
note([26,46,60,70,150,183],'HSRP states include initial, learn, listen, speak, standby and active. Speak sends hellos and participates in election; standby is next in line; active forwards. Initial is not the candidate role described in the false statement.','Learn → listen → speak → standby/active. Read the requested state carefully.')
note([27],'HSRPv2 expands group numbers to 0–4095, compared with 0–255 in version 1, and uses a different virtual MAC range. Priority and preemption already existed in HSRPv1.','Version 2 expands the group-number space.')
note([31,77],'Preemption allows a higher-priority HSRP router to take the active role from a lower-priority active router. Without it, recovering with a higher priority does not automatically reclaim the role.','Priority ranks; preempt reclaims.')
note([34,139],'RADIUS and TACACS+ provide centralized AAA services. In these variants, choose the AAA protocol actually present in the choices; SSH and HTTPS secure connections rather than serving as the central AAA protocol.','Same stem can test a different valid protocol when its choices change.')
note([37,83],'NAC checks endpoint admission and policy compliance. An Email Security Appliance protects email traffic, which is a different role. If the source keys the email appliance for a general endpoint-security question, treat that key as questionable rather than a universal product definition.','Source-key caution: do not memorize Email Security Appliance as a synonym for endpoint admission.')
note([40,41,59,68],'DHCP starvation exhausts the available address pool so legitimate clients cannot obtain leases. DHCP snooping rate limits can restrict abusive DHCP traffic; port security can limit the number of source MAC identities on an access port. The available choices determine which mitigation this variant tests.','Starvation consumes addresses; rogue DHCP gives misleading configuration.')
note([51,62,137,184,187],'Accounting records network use, commands and changes for auditing. Authentication verifies identity and authorization grants permissions; neither replaces the activity record.','Accounting = audit trail: who did what, and when.')
note([53,152],'show port-security interface FastEthernet 0/12 displays operational port-security information for that port. show running-config displays configured commands. Choose the more specific command when offered; the alternate choice set only offers the broader configuration display.','Operational status and saved configuration answer different questions.')
note([55,61,140,147,190],'Port security restricts permitted source MAC addresses. Its default violation mode is shutdown: a violation error-disables the interface and records the event. An attached device with a MAC different from the permitted one can trigger that action.','Shutdown disables; restrict drops and reports; protect drops silently.')
note([56,133],'EtherChannel groups parallel physical links into one logical link, increasing aggregate capacity and providing redundancy. STP treats the bundle as a logical interface, rather than blocking each member as a separate redundant path.','Several cables, one logical port channel.')
note([57,76,191],'BPDU guard disables a protected edge port when it receives a BPDU, preventing an unexpected switch from participating through that port. Enable it per interface, or use the global portfast bpduguard default command for PortFast ports.','PortFast is for endpoints; BPDUs suggest a switch.')
note([58,67],'An HSRP group has one active router and one elected standby at a time; additional routers can listen. The source phrase one or more standby routers is imprecise if interpreted as multiple simultaneously elected standbys.','One active forwards; one elected standby is next.')
note([63,189],'Compare the allowed MAC count and violation mode in the exhibit with the stated requirement. A maximum of one limits the port to one learned source. Shutdown disables a violating port, whereas protect/restrict drop unauthorized frames. These are different behaviors; the shutdown-keyed source variant does not exactly match drop-only wording.','Source-key caution: dropping unauthorized frames is not the same as shutting down the interface.')
note([66,78,144,145,148,180,185],'Port security limits source MAC identities on an access port. Sticky learning adds learned addresses to running configuration; saving that configuration retains them after a reboot. A phone plus a PC needs two permitted addresses; the usual default maximum is one.','Sticky learns into running config; save it to survive restart.')
note([73,80],'HSRP prefers the highest configured priority. Priority 255 is above the default 100; preemption lets that router reclaim active status when available. no shutdown ensures the interface can participate.','High priority + preempt = preferred router can return to active.')
note([75],'A Layer 2 switch needs a default gateway to send management replies to a host on another subnet. Local traffic can work while a remote Telnet session fails because the return route is missing.','Successful local pings do not prove the switch can reply off-subnet.')
note([82,153],'Sticky port security learns the arriving frame’s source MAC as an allowed address. The switch also learns where that source is located for return traffic. With the default maximum of one, a different source can violate security.','Learning uses the source MAC, not the broadcast destination.')
note([84],'Hub-and-spoke connects branches to a central site such as a server farm. It suits traffic concentrated at the center; a full mesh adds many links mainly for direct branch-to-branch paths.','Central resources suggest a central hub.')
note([85,95,172],'Enterprise wireless authentication uses an AAA server, commonly RADIUS, to validate individual user credentials. Personal wireless security instead uses a shared passphrase.','Enterprise = individual identities + authentication server.')
note([86,155],'An access point bridges wireless clients into the LAN. A controller manages APs; a switch primarily connects wired devices. Single or dual radios describe the AP radio hardware.','AP connects clients; WLC coordinates APs.')
note([87,161],'An omnidirectional antenna radiates around its axis, useful for coverage around a central location. A directional antenna concentrates energy toward a chosen area, so calling an omnidirectional antenna focused in one direction is false.','Omni = around; directional = aimed.')
note([88],'CAPWAP carries control messages and encapsulated wireless client traffic between a lightweight AP and its wireless controller. It is not simply encryption between a client and an AP.','CAPWAP joins AP to controller.')
note([89,112,118,164,166],'Select the intended SSID to join the correct WLAN. Change default administrator credentials to protect management; use a strong wireless password to protect access. Renaming an SSID changes identification but is not a substitute for authentication.','SSID = name; Wi-Fi password = entry; admin password = management.')
note([90,108,198],'Broadband wireless access can use technologies such as WiMAX, municipal Wi-Fi and satellite links. These variants present different valid examples; they are not all the same radio technology.','Read the available examples rather than memorizing one answer position.')
note([91,100,116],'IEEE develops LAN/WLAN technical standards; the Wi-Fi Alliance certifies interoperability of Wi-Fi products; the ITU coordinates international spectrum and satellite-orbit use. These organizations serve different roles.','IEEE specifies; Alliance certifies; ITU coordinates spectrum.')
note([92,96,104,123,154,199],'A WLC centrally manages lightweight APs and WLAN settings. On the controller interface in this source, Advanced exposes the full menu and WLANs opens WLAN configuration; a dynamic VLAN interface provides the client network mapping. Menus differ by controller software.','Summary monitors; WLANs configures; interface maps the client network.')
note([93,94,98,102,120,121,122,126,158,162,173,174,196],'802.11b uses 2.4 GHz up to 11 Mb/s; g uses 2.4 GHz up to 54 Mb/s; a uses 5 GHz up to 54 Mb/s. 802.11n supports 2.4/5 GHz and MIMO, with a maximum theoretical 600 Mb/s. 802.11ac uses 5 GHz. Actual throughput is lower than the advertised PHY rate.','b/g = 2.4; a/ac = 5; n = both. Match the feature and standard in this variant.')
note([97],'SNMP is the standard protocol in these choices for monitoring device counters, status and management information. RADIUS provides AAA. This source keys RADIUS, which conflicts with the normal networking definition; the retained study key should not be learned as a technical fact.','Source-key caution: monitoring → SNMP; authentication → RADIUS.')
note([99],'OFDM sends data over many orthogonal subcarriers and is used by several Wi-Fi generations. The broad source wording mostly is simplified: older 802.11b uses a different physical-layer technique.','OFDM divides a channel into parallel subcarriers.')
note([101],'MIMO uses multiple antennas and spatial streams to improve data capacity under suitable radio conditions. It does not mean that one antenna alone transmits more bits merely by increasing power.','Multiple inputs/outputs = multiple antenna paths.')
note([103],'An AP provides wireless access in enterprises or public spaces. Bluetooth support exists on some models, but is not a universal defining AP feature. The source selects the Wi-Fi-and-Bluetooth statement; treat that generalization cautiously.','Source-key caution: an AP is defined by wireless LAN access, not by Bluetooth support.')
note([105,205],'Microwave ovens can interfere with 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi. Channel planning reduces nearby radio interference; 1, 6 and 11 are commonly used non-overlapping 20 MHz channels in the 2.4 GHz plan used by this course.','Separate competing signals in space or frequency.')
note([106,115,124,125,128,157,160,194,197,206],'A WPAN covers a person’s nearby devices, such as Bluetooth. A WLAN covers a local home or office. A WMAN spans a metropolitan area; a WWAN spans a much larger region. Quoted distances are examples, not fixed guarantees.','Personal → local → metropolitan → wide.')
note([107],'Changing a suitable replaceable antenna can improve coverage toward the patio without adding cabling or a second AP. This source assumes an AP with compatible removable antennas; antenna changes do not guarantee coverage through every obstruction.','Improve the radio path before buying another network connection.')
note([109,110,168,202,207],'Distributing compatible clients between 2.4 and 5 GHz can reduce contention; distinct SSIDs can make band selection explicit. However, 802.11b/g-only clients cannot use 5 GHz. The slow-WLAN question has conflicting source keys, so a firmware update and band splitting must not be treated as interchangeable guaranteed fixes.','Source-key caution: verify client band support before moving traffic to 5 GHz.')
note([111,119,170],'An infrastructure WLAN uses APs. Multiple connected basic service sets form an extended service set, allowing a larger coverage area; a distribution system interconnects those service sets.','One cell = BSS; connected cells = ESS.')
note([113,212],'In infrastructure mode a client communicates through an AP. In ad-hoc mode stations communicate directly without an AP. A capable NIC can support either mode depending on its driver and configuration.','Ad hoc = peers directly; infrastructure = AP.')
note([114,159,163,169],'APs announce a WLAN with beacons. In active discovery, a client sends probe requests. If the AP does not advertise the SSID, clients may need to specify it to probe for it. Hiding the SSID is not strong security because management traffic can reveal it.','Beacon announces; probe asks. Hidden name is not encryption.')
note([117,165],'An autonomous AP is configured independently. A lightweight/controller-based AP receives centralized configuration from a WLC. LWAPP is an older controller protocol; CAPWAP is its widely used successor.','Autonomous = self-managed; lightweight = controller-managed.')
note([127],'Quality of Service gives delay-sensitive voice and video preferential handling during congestion. It schedules or prioritizes traffic; it does not create extra bandwidth or replace encryption.','QoS helps urgent packets wait less.')
note([132],'AAA stands for Authentication, Authorization and Accounting. Accuracy is not its third component.','Identity, permissions, recordkeeping.')
note([134],'When a switch cannot learn additional source MAC addresses, traffic to unknown destinations is flooded within its VLAN except back through the ingress port. Existing known destinations can still be forwarded normally; the source phrase all incoming frames is an overgeneralization.','Unknown destination → VLAN flood; known destination → learned port.')
note([141,204],'DHCP snooping distinguishes trusted server-facing paths from untrusted client ports. A rate limit of 6 allows six DHCP messages per second on each selected interface. Count only the required trusted ingress paths in the exhibit, not every client-facing port.','Trusted = server replies allowed; untrusted = clients. Trust depends on traffic direction.')
note([142],'Sticky MAC learning records dynamically learned secure addresses in running configuration. It does not automatically save startup configuration; copying running to startup makes them persistent.','Learn → running; save → startup.')
note([143],'DAI source-MAC validation compares the Ethernet source MAC with the sender MAC inside the ARP message. A mismatch can reveal forged ARP traffic. This is separate from simply looking up a destination in the MAC table.','Outer Ethernet source must match inner ARP sender.')
note([156,193,200],'Satellite links can reach rural areas without terrestrial broadband. Their speed and upload/download ratio depend on the service, so legacy figures in the source are historical examples rather than guarantees for modern satellite providers.','Satellite provides reach; do not assume one fixed speed ratio.')
note([167],'802.11a transmits in 5 GHz, whereas an 802.11g-only AP listens in 2.4 GHz. Being within physical range does not make their radios compatible.','Same location does not mean same frequency.')
note([171],'NAT translates private client addresses to an address usable on the internet. DHCP assigns local addresses, DNS resolves names, and RADIUS authenticates users; those services do not perform address translation.','DHCP gives an address; NAT translates it; DNS finds names.')
note([186],'Protect mode drops frames from unknown source MAC addresses after the allowed maximum is reached, without sending violation notifications. Restrict also reports violations; shutdown disables the port.','Protect = silent drop; restrict = drop + report; shutdown = disable.')
note([192],'HSRP routers send hello messages every three seconds by default to maintain active/standby awareness. The default hold interval is ten seconds; configurable timers can change those values.','Hello 3, hold 10 under default timers.')
note([195],'Many wireless routers expose a browser-based management interface. Access it through its management address and protect it with strong administrator credentials; the browser is the interface, not the authentication method.','Management address + browser + administrator credentials.')
note([201],'Wi-Fi uses CSMA/CA: stations sense the medium and attempt to avoid collisions through waiting and randomized backoff. CSMA/CD is associated with older shared Ethernet and collision detection.','Wireless avoids; shared Ethernet detects.')
note([208],'The RADIUS shared secret establishes trust between the WLC and RADIUS server and protects authentication exchanges, including password handling. Traditional RADIUS does not encrypt every field of every message; the source wording encrypt the messages is simplified.','Shared secret is between controller and AAA server, not a client’s Wi-Fi password.')
note([211],'IEEE 802.11 specifies wireless LAN MAC and physical-layer behavior. IEEE 802.1Q concerns VLAN bridging/tagging and 802.1D covers bridging/spanning-tree concepts, so they are not the Wi-Fi family.','802.11 = WLAN; 802.3 = Ethernet.')
def apply_teaching_notes(notes):
 for i,value in GROUPS:
  if i in [110,168]: value+='\n'+notes.get(str(i),'')
  notes[str(i)]=value
if __name__=='__main__':
 p=ROOT/'summative3-explanations.json'; notes=json.loads(p.read_text(encoding='utf-8'));apply_teaching_notes(notes);p.write_text(json.dumps(notes,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
 print('Concept explanations authored:',len(set(i for i,_ in GROUPS)))
